    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Why do governments choose to challenge the citizenship of ethnic minority groups? Why do some of these cases result in violence, but not others? My research shows that governments choose to challenge the citizenship when the exclusion of the group benefits the government's political project regardless of a democratic transition, and that affected groups will react with violence when there is an intersection of a political opening and a lack of credible exit options. I examined multiple case studies of ethnic minority groups bisected by international borders in sub-Saharan Africa: the Banyarwanda in Congo/Zaire, the Banyarwanda in Uganda, the Dioula in Côte d’Ivoire, the Bamileke in Cameroon, and black Africans in Mauritania. I analyzed key historical points of citizenship contestation and extension to understand the variation of event outcomes within these countries.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Willer, Kirsten. “Citizenship Contestation and Reprisal Violence in Sub-Saharan Africa.” Stanford University, 2017.",
